// error
// #4560: a payload-less variant construction names no type at all, so
// `viaFree(Option.None)` binds nothing to `T`. SPEC §15.3 makes that the CALL's
// error — "if any type parameter cannot be inferred, the call is an error and
// explicit type arguments are required" — and E0068 is the code `vInferEnum`
// already uses for the other context where the expectation is provably absent
// (`let x = Enum.Variant`, validateexpr.bit). `vGenericGap`
// (validategeneric.bit) is the second half.
//
// What this file pins is WHERE the refusal lands. Before it, `bit check` exited
// 0, `genericTypeArgs` fabricated `void` for the unbound `T`, Pass A2 emitted
// `viaFree<void>`, and `bit build` refused with E0092 at `t.label()` — inside a
// body that is correct — which killed the `viaFree(Option.Some(Tag{}))` call in
// the same `main` that had inferred perfectly well. One diagnostic, at the
// `Option.None` call, is the whole point.
//
// The remedies the message names all work and are pinned by
// run_generic_bare_variant_arg.bit. It is not an Option rule: any generic
// enum's payload-less variant reaches this, and that is pinned there too.
interface Named {
  label(): string,
}

class Tag {
  export label(): string {
    return "tag"
  }
}

fn viaFree<T: Named>(q: Option<T>): string {
  match (q) {
    Some(t) => return t.label()
    None => return "none"
  }
}

fn main() {
  print(viaFree(Option.Some(Tag{})) + "\n")
  print(viaFree(Option.None) + "\n")
}
